{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms without smuggling in induction, provided it is framed strictly as a methodological rule based on the asymmetry of risk and informational content rather than a prediction of future truth.","counterconditions":["If Popper explicitly states that choosing a better-corroborated theory for action implies a belief in its higher probability of being true in the future.","If the defense requires attributing any pragmatic advantage or predictive reliability to corroboration that functions identically to inductive probability.","If it can be demonstrated that the 'asymmetry of risk' argument secretly assumes the uniformity of nature (that the future will resemble the past)."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"193e9093bea201f6"},{"block":"02cc3b8a099bb440"},{"block":"182a58bdd39650d7"}],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which corroboration does not alter regarding probability) and the rational choice of which theory to test or act upon. The mechanism relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a regulative ideal and the 'asymmetry of risk': acting on a falsified theory guarantees error, while acting on a severely tested (but not falsified) theory offers the best available, albeit non-probabilistic, guess at closeness to truth. This transforms the preference from an inductive inference about the future to a critical decision procedure grounded in past performance and logical content.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}